<h2>Where the obligations come from</h2>
<p>The same earning member can owe maintenance under several statutes at once, and the obligations are cumulative, not alternative.</p>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Claimant</th><th>Principal provisions</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr><td>Wife</td><td>Section 144 BNSS (formerly Section 125 CrPC); Sections 24 and 25 HMA; Section 18 HAMA; Section 20 DV Act</td><td>Includes a divorced wife who has not remarried, under the BNSS route.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minor children</td><td>Section 144 BNSS; Section 26 HMA; Section 20 HAMA</td><td>Extends to unmarried daughters and, under personal law, to education and marriage expenses.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parents</td><td>Section 144 BNSS; Senior Citizens Act, 2007 (tribunal route)</td><td>Both sons and daughters bear the obligation; the tribunal route is faster but capped by rules.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>How courts actually apportion</h2>
<p>The exercise is one of capacity and reasonableness, not formula. The court begins with the respondent&rsquo;s proven income — tested against the affidavit of assets and liabilities, tax records and lifestyle — then deducts the legitimate claims on it: his own reasonable living expenses, statutory deductions, and genuine support of other dependants. What remains is distributed to give each claimant a standard of living reasonably comparable to what the household enjoyed, with minor children&rsquo;s needs — schooling, medical care — treated as the least compressible component.</p>

<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Step 1 — establish real income</strong>, including income the respondent understates; an earning capacity can be attributed to a respondent who pleads convenient poverty.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Step 2 — identify genuine dependants</strong>, with proof: existing court orders, shared residence, medical bills, school fees.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Step 3 — weigh competing needs</strong>: age and health of parents, number and ages of children, the applicant&rsquo;s own income and earning capacity.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Step 4 — fix the award</strong> so that the aggregate burden is sustainable; an order the respondent cannot possibly obey breeds only arrears and enforcement litigation.</div>
</div>

<h2>Recurring battlegrounds</h2>
<p>Three disputes dominate this terrain. First, the genuineness of claimed dependants: respondents routinely list parents and siblings as dependants while claimants point to the parents&rsquo; pension, property or other earning children. Courts look for actual, demonstrated support. Second, sequencing: a respondent sometimes procures a parents&rsquo; maintenance order — or a voluntary arrangement — after the wife&rsquo;s petition is filed, to shrink the divisible income; the timing itself invites scrutiny. Third, double counting across fora: where a wife holds overlapping awards under the DV Act and the BNSS, adjustment prevents double recovery, but obligations to different claimants are not set off against each other — the wife&rsquo;s award is not reduced simply because the parents also hold one, except through the overall capacity analysis.</p>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>For claimants:</strong> plead the respondent&rsquo;s full income picture and challenge inflated dependant claims with specifics — the parents&rsquo; pension records, the brother&rsquo;s employment, the absence of any actual outgo. <strong>For respondents:</strong> disclose every obligation in the first affidavit, with proof of payment; a liability surfaced late looks invented, while a documented one genuinely moderates quantum.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Maintenance law&rsquo;s purpose is anti-destitution across the whole family, and courts increasingly say so expressly: the wife and children are not rivals of the ageing parents, and the law expects the earning member to carry all of them to the extent his means allow — with the court, not the respondent, deciding what those means are.</p>
</div>
